1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,050
A small garden today. Mint, basil, and a few
radishes.

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:07,270
Small garden. Ambitious gardener.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,210
Then let us give the gardener a plan.

4
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:17,900
Mint? Are we growing money?

5
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:21,410
That would be a very useful garden.

6
00:00:21,660 --> 00:00:24,490
Our shopping list might get smaller.

7
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,050
This mint is an herb. The other mint makes
coins.

8
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:37,270
Two jobs, one word.

9
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:40,780
And only one belongs in our garden.

10
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,050
Mint left, basil in the middle, radishes on
the right.

11
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:52,270
Three pots. Three saucers.

12
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:55,780
And three labels that tell the truth.

13
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:03,760
My brother Ben is bringing the pots.

14
00:01:04,010 --> 00:01:05,810
Empty ones?

15
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:10,610
Yes. We are supplying the plants, not ordering
a forest.

16
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,900
Labels and gloves are ready.

17
00:01:18,150 --> 00:01:20,980
Do the seeds need tiny gloves?

18
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:23,630
No. Just our careful hands.

19
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,900
I will bring their drink.

20
00:01:33,150 --> 00:01:37,700
Bring the can first. We will check the soil
outside.

21
00:01:37,950 --> 00:01:40,780
No straws for the basil, then.

22
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:50,480
Stay on the clear path with your hands free of
soil.

23
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:53,990
Even my grand plan is walking carefully.

24
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:57,070
That is a good first step.

25
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:02,900
Three empty homes, as promised.

26
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:06,410
Excellent. Their new residents are already
waiting.

27
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,920
Hello, Ben. Thank you for bringing them.

28
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:19,190
Three pots and three saucers. Nothing growing
yet.

29
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,840
Not even a surprise tree.

30
00:02:22,090 --> 00:02:24,920
That keeps our small plan small.

31
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:33,330
We nearly ordered a money plant.

32
00:02:33,580 --> 00:02:36,840
Dan once labeled an empty box “treasure.”

33
00:02:37,090 --> 00:02:39,490
It contained very valuable possibilities.

34
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:48,760
Enjoy your garden. Label first, guess later.

35
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:51,840
A useful family motto. Thanks, Ben.

36
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:55,780
We will show you the journal next time.

37
00:03:00,500 --> 00:03:03,760
There is a hole in the bottom.

38
00:03:04,010 --> 00:03:06,840
Extra water needs a way out.

39
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:10,350
A pot with an exit. Sensible architecture.

40
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,330
Five hundred milliliters in the can.

41
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:20,550
That sounds satisfyingly official.

42
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,780
It is a record, not an instruction to use it
all.

43
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:32,900
Those two already have leaves.

44
00:03:33,150 --> 00:03:37,270
They are young plants. The radishes start as
seeds.

45
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:40,350
Different beginnings, the same careful
attention.

46
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,900
Tools here. Clear path there.

47
00:03:48,150 --> 00:03:50,550
And nobody swings the shovel.

48
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,770
Especially our ambitious gardener.

49
00:04:00,500 --> 00:04:04,620
Left for mint, middle for basil, right for
radishes.

50
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:07,700
Each pot gets its own saucer.

51
00:04:07,950 --> 00:04:11,210
Now the bench has a clear plan.

52
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:17,470
Can my coin help?

53
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,410
It can remind us which mint we mean.

54
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:24,490
A very profitable piece of paper.

55
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,330
Gloves on, hands away from mouths.

56
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:36,840
We will wash properly when we finish.

57
00:04:37,090 --> 00:04:41,640
The plants are not the only things getting
good care.

58
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:48,330
It is already a little damp.

59
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:51,840
Yes. We will check before adding water.

60
00:04:52,090 --> 00:04:55,350
Slowly keeps the soil where it belongs.

61
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:02,900
Leave room at the top?

62
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:06,410
Yes. Room for water without spilling soil.

63
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:10,350
Even a pot appreciates a little breathing
space.

64
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,900
One generous drink for everyone?

65
00:05:18,150 --> 00:05:22,270
Check first. Damp soil does not need a flood.

66
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:26,640
Let us not give the roots a swimming pool.

67
00:05:30,500 --> 00:05:33,330
It looks much greener than money.

68
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:36,410
Today we are planting, not tasting.

69
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:39,920
Its leaves are the better clue anyway.

70
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:48,330
The tag says mint. Left pot.

71
00:05:48,580 --> 00:05:51,410
I checked the label before guessing.

72
00:05:51,660 --> 00:05:54,920
A strong start for our ambitious gardener.

73
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:04,620
You are holding the roots, not tugging the
leaves.

74
00:06:04,870 --> 00:06:06,840
Exactly. Gentle hands underneath.

75
00:06:07,090 --> 00:06:10,350
No plant gets pulled by its hairstyle.

76
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,470
It has moved house.

77
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:19,690
Without changing its name.

78
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:23,200
Now we support the roots with soil.

79
00:06:30,500 --> 00:06:32,900
Like tucking in a blanket?

80
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:35,980
Gently. The roots still need air.

81
00:06:36,230 --> 00:06:38,030
Comfortable, not squashed.

82
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:49,620
One empty nursery pot, ready to use another
day.

83
00:06:49,870 --> 00:06:53,990
So the plant has not left a twin behind.

84
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:56,210
Only its old address.

85
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,470
The leaves look different.

86
00:07:02,720 --> 00:07:05,550
This is basil. Its tag agrees.

87
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,630
Middle pot, just as we planned.

88
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:17,300
Another moving day.

89
00:07:17,550 --> 00:07:19,950
And the same gentle lift.

90
00:07:20,200 --> 00:07:22,600
Our garden has excellent movers.

91
00:07:30,500 --> 00:07:32,470
Basil in the middle.

92
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,980
Soil around the roots, below the rim.

93
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:39,490
I have its label ready for later.

94
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:48,330
Not too tight around the stem.

95
00:07:48,580 --> 00:07:51,840
That is right. Support it without squeezing.

96
00:07:52,090 --> 00:07:55,350
A comfortable fit, even without shoe sizes.

97
00:08:00,500 --> 00:08:03,330
Which plant is in the middle?

98
00:08:03,580 --> 00:08:08,130
Basil. The mint is not making change on the
left.

99
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:11,640
Good. Our garden names are staying put.

100
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:17,900
This pot looks rather empty.

101
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:20,550
It is ready, not forgotten.

102
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:23,200
Its residents are much smaller.

103
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,900
Three seeds for this pot.

104
00:08:33,150 --> 00:08:36,410
The rest stay dry in their packet.

105
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:40,780
A tiny beginning does not need a crowded home.

106
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,760
Space between them, and not too deep.

107
00:08:49,010 --> 00:08:52,700
This packet tells us what these seeds need.

108
00:08:52,950 --> 00:08:54,920
No basement apartments, then.

109
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:02,470
One in the back.

110
00:09:02,720 --> 00:09:04,520
Two still waiting.

111
00:09:04,770 --> 00:09:08,030
They will all have their own space.

112
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:17,470
One for this space.

113
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:19,690
A very orderly neighborhood.

114
00:09:19,940 --> 00:09:22,770
And one neighbor still to come.

115
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,900
The last neighbor has arrived.

116
00:09:33,150 --> 00:09:35,550
And the dish is empty.

117
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:39,060
Now we can cover all three gently.

118
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:47,470
Where are the radishes?

119
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:50,550
Below the soil. They need time.

120
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:54,920
Then our first picture can be a quiet pot.

121
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,190
Label first, guess later. Ben had a point.

122
00:10:04,440 --> 00:10:06,410
Three names, three stakes.

123
00:10:06,660 --> 00:10:09,920
Let the handwriting agree with the plants.

124
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:17,470
Mint. The leafy kind.

125
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:20,550
That label will help us remember.

126
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:24,060
My wallet can stop expecting a harvest.

127
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,470
Basil in the middle.

128
00:10:32,720 --> 00:10:34,690
Name and place match.

129
00:10:34,940 --> 00:10:38,200
And the original tag agrees with both.

130
00:10:45,500 --> 00:10:48,760
The name is taller than the plant.

131
00:10:49,010 --> 00:10:50,810
For today.

132
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:54,750
It will remind us what is growing underneath.

133
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:03,760
Day one: planted. No radish shoots yet.

134
00:11:04,010 --> 00:11:07,270
An honest report from a quiet pot.

135
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:10,780
That is how a useful journal begins.

136
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:18,760
Perhaps the mint wants the whole can.

137
00:11:19,010 --> 00:11:23,130
Let us ask the soil before making that offer.

138
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:26,640
It has already declined the swimming pool.

139
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:34,190
Start with one small measured amount, then
observe.

140
00:11:34,440 --> 00:11:36,410
One hundred, not everything.

141
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:39,920
Now the can has four hundred left.

142
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:48,330
A small drink, around the roots.

143
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:50,380
No swimming lessons.

144
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:53,890
Now we watch what the water does.

145
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,330
A little came through the bottom.

146
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:07,700
The soil has settled. That is enough for now.

147
00:12:07,950 --> 00:12:10,780
Stopping is part of watering too.

148
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:20,050
Three hundred in the can, one hundred in the
cup.

149
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:23,990
Our water is getting a very careful
accountant.

150
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,500
And the basil gets a careful drink.

151
00:12:30,500 --> 00:12:32,900
Same small measure, different plant.

152
00:12:33,150 --> 00:12:35,980
We still check its own soil.

153
00:12:36,230 --> 00:12:41,210
The amount is our record, not a rule for every
day.

154
00:12:45,500 --> 00:12:49,190
Do all pots want the same drink forever?

155
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:53,560
No. Weather, roots, soil and pot size change
that.

156
00:12:53,810 --> 00:12:56,640
We will check again another day.

157
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:03,760
One hundred for the seed pot, gently.

158
00:13:04,010 --> 00:13:08,130
A slow pour will keep the soil in place.

159
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:10,780
The tiny neighbors dislike waterfalls.

160
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:17,300
Nothing green yet.

161
00:13:17,550 --> 00:13:21,670
That is normal. We planted seeds, not a
shortcut.

162
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:25,180
Our journal can wait for real changes.

163
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:35,050
Two hundred left. I do not have to spend it.

164
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:38,130
Exactly. Use what the plants need.

165
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:41,640
Three small drinks, not one grand flood.

166
00:13:45,500 --> 00:13:48,330
Three hundred used. Two hundred left.

167
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:50,380
Together, five hundred.

168
00:13:50,630 --> 00:13:55,180
And tomorrow we check the soil before choosing
any amount.

169
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:02,900
No pools under the houses.

170
00:14:03,150 --> 00:14:06,410
Roots need drainage, not a permanent bath.

171
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:09,490
The swimming club is officially closed.

172
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:18,330
The tree makes shade over there.

173
00:14:18,580 --> 00:14:21,410
These tags ask for good light.

174
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:26,640
This sunny strip gives us room to check the
pots safely.

175
00:14:30,500 --> 00:14:32,470
Left pot, left place.

176
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:35,120
The label faces the path.

177
00:14:35,370 --> 00:14:38,200
And the walking space stays clear.

178
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:47,900
Basil in the middle again.

179
00:14:48,150 --> 00:14:50,550
It has kept its address.

180
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:54,920
And both of you have kept the path clear.

181
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:02,470
Radishes on the right.

182
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:05,980
A quiet home, with a clear name.

183
00:15:06,230 --> 00:15:10,350
Now we can watch without digging up the
answer.

184
00:15:15,500 --> 00:15:19,190
Two leafy plants and one pot of waiting.

185
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:22,700
The seed positions go in the diagram.

186
00:15:22,950 --> 00:15:28,360
A record can show what we know and what we can
see.

187
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:34,190
Can the seeds stay out of the rain?

188
00:15:34,440 --> 00:15:38,130
The spare packet goes on the dry shelf.

189
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:41,640
Our tools get a home as well.

190
00:15:45,500 --> 00:15:48,330
Two hundred saved, not secretly added.

191
00:15:48,580 --> 00:15:50,980
My grand gesture can wait.

192
00:15:51,230 --> 00:15:54,920
Today your careful stopping was the useful
part.

193
00:16:00,500 --> 00:16:02,470
Gloves stay out here.

194
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:05,550
Yes. We still wash our hands.

195
00:16:05,800 --> 00:16:11,210
No soil goes on the door handle if we can help
it.

196
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:18,330
Before a snack, a proper wash.

197
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,550
Even the gardener’s accountant?

198
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:24,060
Especially anyone with soil on their hands.

199
00:16:30,500 --> 00:16:32,470
Day one is complete.

200
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:36,410
And the paper coin is still above ground.

201
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:39,490
Exactly where a pretend coin belongs.

202
00:16:45,500 --> 00:16:48,760
Will the radishes be ready after lunch?

203
00:16:49,010 --> 00:16:52,700
No. We will watch over the coming days.

204
00:16:52,950 --> 00:16:56,210
Then patience belongs on the supply list.

205
00:17:00,500 --> 00:17:03,760
I will leave room for what changes.

206
00:17:04,010 --> 00:17:06,840
We cannot write the answer yet.

207
00:17:07,090 --> 00:17:11,640
That is a good reason to come back and look.

208
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:19,620
Your paper coin grew into a very good joke.

209
00:17:19,870 --> 00:17:21,670
And a reminder.

210
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:23,890
Both are worth keeping.

211
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:33,330
A last look from the path.

212
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:36,410
No extra drink, no extra seed.

213
00:17:36,660 --> 00:17:39,490
Just the garden we actually planted.

214
00:17:45,500 --> 00:17:47,900
I nearly wanted a forest.

215
00:17:48,150 --> 00:17:51,840
Three pots give us room to notice things.

216
00:17:52,090 --> 00:17:55,350
And time to care for them together.

217
00:18:00,500 --> 00:18:03,330
We will visit again, tiny neighbors.

218
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:06,410
Quiet neighbors are easy to hear.

219
00:18:06,660 --> 00:18:10,780
Especially when Dad is not giving a gardening
speech.

220
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:20,050
I wanted to be helpful. Checking helped more
than pouring.

221
00:18:20,300 --> 00:18:23,130
You listened and changed the plan.

222
00:18:23,380 --> 00:18:25,780
The roots probably appreciate that.

223
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:34,190
Labels, a drawing, a water record. Everyone
helped.

224
00:18:34,440 --> 00:18:38,130
Even a quiet pot has a story now.

225
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:40,780
A very short first chapter.

226
00:18:45,500 --> 00:18:50,050
We can tell everyone about our garden at the
gathering.

227
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:53,130
A true story. No money harvest.

228
00:18:53,380 --> 00:18:57,070
And no promise that the radishes will hurry.

229
00:19:00,500 --> 00:19:04,190
Our garden is not bigger than the house.

230
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:06,240
A wise limit.

231
00:19:06,490 --> 00:19:10,610
It still has enough names to keep you busy.

232
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:18,760
Herbs upright. Radish soil bare. Saucers
empty.

233
00:19:19,010 --> 00:19:20,980
A surprisingly satisfying report.

234
00:19:21,230 --> 00:19:24,060
Because it says what really happened.

235
00:19:30,500 --> 00:19:33,760
Three pots, six helpers, one better plan.

236
00:19:34,010 --> 00:19:36,410
And a morning well spent.

237
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:39,920
The mint is still not making change.

238
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:49,190
We will come back and see what changes.

239
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:51,240
Together.

240
00:19:51,490 --> 00:19:53,460
I can wait. Mostly.
